{"name":"Cactus","hex":"#5b6f55","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Cactus","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A taxonomic genus within the family Cactaceae – original Linnean genus of cactus, rejected in 1905."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A city in Texas.","An unincorporated community in California."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Cactus"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cactus","blocks":[{"text":"A cactus is a member of the plant family Cactaceae, a family of the order Caryophyllales comprising about 131 genera with some 1,866 known species. The word cactus derives, through Latin, from the Ancient Greek word κάκτος (káktos), a name originally used by Theophrastus for a spiny plant whose identity is now not certain. Cacti occur in a wide range of shapes and sizes. They are native to the Americas, ranging from Patagonia in the south to parts of western Canada in the north, with the exception of Rhipsalis baccifera, which is also found in Africa and Sri Lanka. Cacti are adapted to live in very dry environments, including the Atacama Desert, one of the driest places on Earth. Because of this, cacti show many adaptations to conserve water. For example, almost all cacti are succulents, meaning they have thickened, fleshy organs adapted to store water. Unlike many other succulents, the stem is the only part of most cacti where water storage takes place. Most species of cacti have lost true leaves, retaining only spines, which are highly modified leaves. As well as defending against herbivores, spines help prevent water loss by reducing air flow close to the cactus and providing some shade. In the absence of true leaves, cacti's enlarged stems carry out photosynthesis."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Various_Cactaceae.jpg/330px-Various_Cactaceae.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cactus"},{"title":"Cactus (American band)","blocks":[{"text":"Cactus is an American rock band formed in 1969. The lineup of 1970 and 1971, consisting of bassist Tim Bogert, drummer Carmine Appice, guitarist Jim McCarty and singer Rusty Day, released three albums. A new lineup of Bogert, Appice, guitarist Werner Fritzschings, keyboardist Duane Hitchings on keyboards and singer Peter French released the band's fourth album in 1972. The band currently comprises Jimmy Kunes as lead singer, guitarist Paul Warren, drummer Appice, bassist Jimmy Caputo and Randy Pratt on harmonica."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Cactus_%281970%29.png/330px-Cactus_%281970%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cactus_(American_band)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Singapore_Botanic_Gardens_Cactus_Garden_2.jpg/500px-Singapore_Botanic_Gardens_Cactus_Garden_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Singapore_Botanic_Gardens_Cactus_Garden_2.jpg/1280px-Singapore_Botanic_Gardens_Cactus_Garden_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Singapore_Botanic_Gardens_Cactus_Garden_2.jpg/500px-Singapore_Botanic_Gardens_Cactus_Garden_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASingapore_Botanic_Gardens_Cactus_Garden_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Cactus1web.jpg/500px-Cactus1web.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Cactus1web.jpg/500px-Cactus1web.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACactus1web.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Barrel_cactus_with_a_view.JPG/500px-Barrel_cactus_with_a_view.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Barrel_cactus_with_a_view.JPG/1280px-Barrel_cactus_with_a_view.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Barrel_cactus_with_a_view.JPG/500px-Barrel_cactus_with_a_view.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ferocactus species, a cactoid, in its native Arizona habitat","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABarrel_cactus_with_a_view.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Arrojadoa_luetzelburgii_4.jpeg/500px-Arrojadoa_luetzelburgii_4.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Arrojadoa_luetzelburgii_4.jpeg/1280px-Arrojadoa_luetzelburgii_4.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Arrojadoa_luetzelburgii_4.jpeg/500px-Arrojadoa_luetzelburgii_4.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Aberrant form of Lagenosocereus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArrojadoa_luetzelburgii_4.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Pereskia_aculeata5.jpg/500px-Pereskia_aculeata5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Pereskia_aculeata5.jpg/960px-Pereskia_aculeata5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Pereskia_aculeata5.jpg/500px-Pe